    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d681">

                <pb facs="0646" n="638" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg638"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6359 Wolfram Ore/72: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-29T09:00:00+07:00">January 29, 1940—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 29—4:53 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>51. Reference Department’s mail instruction 176, September 12, 1939,<anchor
                        n="39" xml:id="d681-f1"/> wolfram exports.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The person referred to<anchor n="40" xml:id="d681-f2"/> in line 9, page 1,
                        of the Embassy’s despatch No. 331, September 22, 1939,<anchor n="41"
                            xml:id="d681-f3"/> informed a member of the staff of the Embassy January
                        17 that Sino-Soviet relations continued satisfactory, but that some concern
                        was felt by Chinese over the fact <pb facs="0647" n="639" xml:id="pg639"/>
                        that the British and French were exerting pressure to have the Chinese limit
                        shipments of wolfram to Russia to amounts which would preclude the
                        possibility of reshipment of any to Germany; that Chinese had declined to
                        comply with French request to make shipments to Russia on a “quota” basis;
                        that Chinese preferred to ship to Russia since credits bring ten times the
                        value of goods exported in the form of goods received in exchange whereas
                        sales to French and British bring only the actual value of exports. It
                        seemed likely that French and British would continue to insist on guarantee
                        that no wolfram stocks exported from Indo-China and/or Hong Kong were
                        destined ultimately for Germany, but informant seemed hopeful of a solution
                        which would not affect barter trade with Soviets adversely.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>It was learned from the British Embassy on January 26 that the British
                        have proposed to the French and American Governments a plan whereby the
                        three countries would purchase the entire production of wolfram.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping, Hong Kong, Shanghai. Hong Kong please inform Hanoi.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
